<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Ichalkaranji State</h1><p class="st-meta">1696 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Ichalkaranji</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Maratha jagir of the Deccan held by the Ghorpade family under Kolhapur, its town now a major textile centre.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ichalkaranji was granted at the end of the 17th century to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naro-mahadev">Naro Mahadev</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> officer whose descendants took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghorpade">Ghorpade</a>. The family held it as a feudatory of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kolhapur-state">Kolhapur State</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The chiefs ruled a small territory on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panchganga-river">Panchganga</a> river, subordinate to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kolhapur-state">Kolhapur</a> and under British supervision through the Kolhapur residency. In the early 20th century the town began to develop handloom and powerloom weaving.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ichalkaranji merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bombay-presidency">Bombay Province</a> in 1949 and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> in 1960. It is now one of Maharashtra's leading textile towns, in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kolhapur-state">Kolhapur</a> district.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1696 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.45%;width:5.03%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class=